My Problem statement:

Time-strapped partners in dual-income households end up planning most of their household's date nights — and pay a real cost for it. A typical Singapore couple split across the island (one starting in the East, the other in the West, meeting somewhere in between) spends ~30 minutes per session juggling browser tabs and Google Maps to find something fresh, open, and fair on travel time. Most of the city's pop-ups and limited-run experiences slip past them. The fallback — a familiar restaurant near home — feels safe but uninspired, and over time signals less care than the planner actually intends.

How might we (HMW) compress 30 minutes of fragmented date-night research into a 60-second decision — surfacing time-sensitive options that are open at the chosen time and fair to both partners' commutes?

Why now: Singapore's F&B and events scene rewards early movers — pop-ups, limited-run concepts, and ticketed shows often sell out within days of opening. Couples without a real-time signal default to evergreen venues and miss the city's most distinctive moments.


